  <h1>Zhan Zhuang Basics: Start Here</h1>

  <h2>What is Zhan Zhuang?</h2>
  <p>Zhan Zhuang (literally "standing like a post"), also known as "standing like a tree" or "standing qigong,"
  is a traditional Chinese qigong practice that is <strong>outwardly still yet inwardly active</strong>.
  You hold specific standing postures and, through the coordination of posture, breath, and mental focus,
  cultivate internal energy (chi) to build strength and improve health.</p>

  <p>As Master Lam Kam Chuen says: <em>"Stand like a tree — the tree appears still on the outside, yet inside it is growing slowly and powerfully."</em></p>

  <h2>Why Practice Zhan Zhuang?</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>💪 Build Strength</h3>
      <p>Improve stamina, endurance, and balance; correct posture; reduce the risk of falls.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🧘 Relieve Stress</h3>
      <p>Ease work pressure, calm the mind, and improve sleep quality.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>⚡ Boost Energy</h3>
      <p>Recharge through stillness — more effective at restoring vitality than exercise or rest alone.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🌿 Gentle & Safe</h3>
      <p>No impact, low intensity — suitable for all ages and fitness levels.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Core Principles of Zhan Zhuang</h2>
  <p>The philosophical foundation of Zhan Zhuang stems from the Daoist concept of "wu wei" —
  <strong>outwardly doing nothing, yet inwardly engaged in the transformation and accumulation of energy</strong>.</p>

  <p>Professor Yu Yongnian (Master Lam's teacher) explains from a modern medical perspective:
  Zhan Zhuang rests the cerebral cortex through conscious mental inhibition, improves microcirculation by
  fine-tuning posture, dilates capillaries, promotes metabolism, and strengthens the immune system.</p>

  <p>The core elements of Zhan Zhuang can be summarized as the "Three Regulations":</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Regulate Body (Tiao Shen)</th><th>Regulate Breath (Tiao Xi)</th><th>Regulate Mind (Tiao Xin)</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Align the skeleton, specific postures</td>
      <td>Natural breathing, deep and silent</td>
      <td>Mental focus guidance, relax into stillness</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Lam's System: Five Postures</h2>
  <p>Lam Kam Chuen's system includes 5 core postures, progressing from simple to complex:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/postures/wu-chi"><strong>Wu Chi</strong></a> — The foundation of foundations, arms hanging naturally</li>
    <li><a href="/postures/holding-balloon"><strong>Holding the Balloon</strong></a> — Arms cradling at heart height</li>
    <li><a href="/postures/holding-front"><strong>Holding in Front</strong></a> — Arms extended forward, palms down</li>
    <li><a href="/postures/splitting-water"><strong>Splitting Water</strong></a> — Arms at the sides of the body</li>
    <li><a href="/postures/holding-dantian"><strong>Holding at Dantian</strong></a> — Arms cradling a ball in front of the Dantian</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Complete Practice Sequence</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <h4>⚠️ Do not skip any stage</h4>
    <p><strong>Warm-up → Zhan Zhuang practice → Cool-down</strong></p>
    <p>Warm-ups open the meridians and release tension; standing practice cultivates energy;
    cool-downs guide energy back to its proper place. Skipping the cool-down leaves energy scattered and uncollected.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notes for Beginners</h2>
  <ul class="key-points">
    <li>Start with <strong>3-5 minutes</strong> and gradually increase the duration</li>
    <li>Do not skip ahead — do not move to the next posture until you can comfortably hold the current one for 20 minutes</li>
    <li>Soreness, numbness, swelling, sweating, and tingling in the fingers are <strong>normal reactions</strong></li>
    <li>Stop immediately if you feel <strong>dizzy or overheated</strong></li>
    <li>Daily consistency is more effective than occasional long sessions</li>
    <li>Wear comfortable clothing and practice in a quiet, well-ventilated space</li>
  </ul>

